    which is similar to matrix, however, unlike matrices,
    each row may contain a different number of columns.
    Given lst, and integer x, find integers x in the list,
    and return list of lists, [[x1, y1], [x2, y2] ...] such that
    each list is a coordinate - (row, columns), starting with 0.
    Sort coordinates initially by rows in ascending order.
    Also, sort coordinates of the row by columns in descending order.

    Examples:
    getRow([
      [1,2,3,4,5,6],
      [1,2,3,4,1,6],
      [1,2,3,4,5,1]
    ], 1) == [[0, 0], [1, 4], [1, 0], [2, 5], [2, 0]]
    getRow([], 1) == []
    getRow([[], [1], [1, 2, 3]], 3) == [[2, 2]]
     */
    public List<List<Integer>> getRow(List<List<Integer>> lst, int x) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        // Handle null or empty input
        if (lst == null || lst.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        // Iterate through rows in ascending order (0 to N-1)
        for (int r = 0; r < lst.size(); r++) {
            List<Integer> row = lst.get(r);
            if (row == null) continue;

            // To ensure columns are in descending order for the same row,
            // we iterate through the columns from the end of the row to the start.
            for (int c = row.size() - 1; c >= 0; c--) {
                // Use .equals() for Integer objects to avoid issues with object reference comparison
                if (row.get(c) != null && row.get(c).equals(x)) {
                    // Create a coordinate pair [row, col]
                    List<Integer> coordinate = new ArrayList<>();
                    coordinate.add(r);
                    coordinate.add(c);
                    result.add(coordinate);
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
